    "problem_statement": "get_next_run() ignores tag parameter and returns wrong job\n\nWhen using `get_next_run()` with a tag parameter, the method ignores the tag filtering and returns the next run time from all jobs instead of just the tagged ones. This breaks tag-based scheduling functionality.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport schedule\n\ndef job1():\n    print(\"Job 1\")\n\ndef job2():\n    print(\"Job 2\")\n\ndef job3():\n    print(\"Job 3\")\n\n# Schedule jobs with different tags and intervals\nschedule.every(5).seconds.do(job1).tag('tag1')\nschedule.every(2).hours.do(job2).tag('tag1', 'tag2') \nschedule.every(1).minutes.do(job3).tag('tag1', 'tag3', 'tag2')\n\n# This should return the next run time for jobs with 'tag2' only\n# Expected: job3's next run time (1 minute job)\n# Actual: returns the latest scheduled job instead of earliest tagged job\nprint(\"Next run for tag2:\", schedule.default_scheduler.get_next_run('tag2'))\nprint(\"Next run for tag3:\", schedule.next_run('tag3'))\n```\n\nThe method returns the wrong next run time when filtering by tags, making it impossible to properly track when specific tagged jobs will execute next."
